    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d303">

                <pb facs="0334" n="252" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg252"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.001G29/15.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the British
                        Ambassador.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-03-18"><hi rendition="#italic">March 18,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1111.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: Referring to your No. 28 of the 17th
                    ultimo, extending an invitation from your Government to the Government of the
                    United States to have a man-of-war present at the naval review to be held at
                    Spithead on Saturday the 24th of June on the occasion of <pb facs="0335" n="253"
                        xml:id="pg253"/> the coronation of His Britannic Majesty, George V, I take
                    pleasure in informing you of the receipt of a letter from the Secretary of the
                    Navy, dated March 9, 1911,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d303-f1"/> in which he states
                    that the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Delaware</hi> has been designated to
                    represent this Government on that occasion.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington
                    Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
